::JOUR_U05_UA_Q31::
Question ID: JOUR_U05_UA_Q31
[MLA.JOUR.DIG.01] Source: Presentation Plan: Transit Safety Report. Reporting Topic: Students crossing the east parking lot after dismissal. Source Notes: The school safety aide counted thirty-seven students crossing between parked cars on Tuesday. Two students said the marked crosswalk is hard to see when buses are lined up. The assistant principal said the school can add temporary cones while it reviews permanent signs. Presentation Goal: Explain the safety concern clearly and recommend a short-term solution without exaggerating the problem. Draft Slide Sequence: Slide 1 headline and central finding. Slide 2 photo of the crossing area with caption. Slide 3 key evidence from the count and student comments. Slide 4 recommendation for cones and clearer crosswalk markings. Slide 5 source note and next step. Question 1: Which slide should introduce the central finding? {
~A. Slide 1 should include every interview note from the report.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
=B. Slide 1 should introduce the central finding so the audience knows the report purpose.#Correct. Step 1: identify the production or presentation skill in the standard. Step 2: connect the choice to the embedded journalism source. This answer works because it supports coherent reporting, accurate visual communication, clear sequence, audience understanding, or credible evidence.
~C. Slide 1 should use only a decorative title with no finding.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
~D. Slide 1 should start with the final source note only.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
}

::JOUR_U05_UA_Q32::
Question ID: JOUR_U05_UA_Q32
[MLA.JOUR.COM.01] Source: Presentation Plan: Transit Safety Report. Reporting Topic: Students crossing the east parking lot after dismissal. Source Notes: The school safety aide counted thirty-seven students crossing between parked cars on Tuesday. Two students said the marked crosswalk is hard to see when buses are lined up. The assistant principal said the school can add temporary cones while it reviews permanent signs. Presentation Goal: Explain the safety concern clearly and recommend a short-term solution without exaggerating the problem. Draft Slide Sequence: Slide 1 headline and central finding. Slide 2 photo of the crossing area with caption. Slide 3 key evidence from the count and student comments. Slide 4 recommendation for cones and clearer crosswalk markings. Slide 5 source note and next step. Question 2: Which evidence best supports the safety concern? {
~A. The strongest evidence is that parking lots are always unsafe.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
